CompTIA Security 2.1


The six threat actors:

Nation-state. Government-backed, huge budgets, very skilled, patient.
Unskilled attacker. Uses other people’s tools without really understanding them. (Older material calls them “script kiddies.”)
Hacktivist. Attacks for a cause or a political message.
Insider threat. Someone already inside: an employee, contractor, or ex-employee.
Organised crime. It’s a business, and the goal is money.
Shadow IT. Staff using unapproved apps or devices. Not usually malicious, but it creates risk.

1. Most dangerous: Nation-states have the biggest budgets, the most skill and the most patience. One term to listen for in the video is APT (Advanced Persistent Threat). It describes an attacker who gets in quietly and stays hidden for months or years, and it’s strongly linked to nation-states. Organised crime is run like a business, with real funding and skilled people, and its motive is almost always money. An insider might have few resources but a huge advantage in access and knowledge. 

2. Insider threat: This is my strongest one I got, and the bank examples are exactly the kind of real experience that will make your blog posts stand out. You hit the key points: insiders are trusted, they already have access, and sometimes they have admin rights. At the bank as Tech there was always someone asking “can you just check this printer?” with out a ticket and usually by the time you get back you fixed the printer and everyones browser setup and questions maybe not all of it gets writen up the way it should. It’s a real gap, because work done without a ticket leaves no audit trail, so nobody can see what was done or why. This leads into defences for study later, like least privilege, separation of duties and logging.

3. Shadow IT: My example (forgetting to log off at ANZ using admin account on another users pc) is  under accidental insider threat or misconfiguration. Shadow IT is more specific:

Shadow IT is hardware, software or cloud services that staff use without IT knowing about or approving them. Some examples:

Saving work files to a personal Google Drive or Dropbox
Plugging their own Wi-Fi router into the office network to get better signal
Pasting customer data into a free online tool or AI chatbot

The key problem is that IT can’t protect what it doesn’t know exists. Nobody patches it, monitors it, backs it up or secures it. Think of the router example as a staff member quietly adding a side door to the building. They just wanted an easier way in, but security doesn’t know the door is there, so nobody locks it.

One more point: shadow IT usually isn’t caused by careless people. It’s often well-meaning staff trying to get their work done faster, because the approved tools are slow or clunky. That’s partly why it’s so common.

OVERVIEW OF THE MAIN THREAT ATTACKERS ABOVE
Nation-states usually aren’t money-motivated. Their main motives are espionage, war, political goals and disruption, like stealing government secrets or attacking another country’s power grid. There’s one well-known exception: North Korea steals cryptocurrency to fund its government. As a general rule, though, organised crime = money and nation-state = espionage and power.
Insider motives. Espionage as stealing secrets for a competitor, not protecting against one. The big insider motive you missed is revenge, like a disgruntled employee who was passed over or is about to be fired. Money is another one.
Unskilled attacker. Add their motive: usually chaos, disruption or bragging rights. They’re still dangerous, because the tools they borrow can be very powerful.
Organised crime competing with other gangs is true in real life, but it isn’t a Security+ point. For the exam, remember: well-funded, skilled, run like a business, and after money (ransomware is their big earner).

Actor	Internal / External	Resources	Sophistication	Typical motive
Nation-state	External	Very high	Very high	Espionage, war, politics
Organised crime	External	High	High	Money
Hacktivist	External	Varies	Varies	Political or philosophical beliefs
Unskilled attacker	External	Low	Low	Chaos, bragging
Insider threat	Internal	Varies	Varies (but has access)	Revenge, money, espionage
Shadow IT	Internal	Low	Low	Convenience (not malicious)

APT (Advanced Persistent Threat):

Advanced: skilled and well-funded
Persistent: stays for months or years
Threat: targeting something valuable, like weapons designs

Think of it like a burglar who gets into your roof cavity and lives there quietly for 18 months, coming down at night to photocopy your files. They’re in the house, but they were never invited in. An insider is someone who was given a key.
